# Cloudflare Pages headers. This file is what actually applies here.
#
# The deploy is a DIRECT UPLOAD of an assembled folder (`wrangler pages deploy _site`),
# so Pages never reads the repository and never runs a build. A `_headers` file
# sitting in the uploaded directory is the documented, unambiguous way to attach
# headers to a file-based deploy.
#
# THIS FILE USED TO SAY NETLIFY, and the correction is worth leaving in rather
# than quietly overwriting: the project moved to Cloudflare Pages on 2026-08-03
# over Netlify's non-refilling free-tier credits, and this comment described the
# old host for a week and a half. Nothing was broken by it — the two use
# byte-identical `_headers` syntax, which is exactly why the move needed no
# change here and exactly why the staleness was invisible. A file that ships
# with the site and describes a host we left is the same defect as a checklist
# that disagrees with the deploy log.
#
# tools/build-site.js copies it in by name, like every other shipped file.

# The built set is a dated snapshot, REPLACED on the 25-day refresh rather than
# edited, and the biggest thing a visitor downloads: 15,890 cards, 4.6MB of JSON
# compressing to ~1.6MB over the wire. A day of caching means a returning player
# pays that once. It must never exceed the refresh cadence, or a browser could
# serve a snapshot older than the 30-day policy cap allows.
/sets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

# The app's own code is small and changes whenever a fix ships. Pages serves it
# with an ETag, so `no-cache` means "revalidate", not "re-download" — a 304 costs
# a round trip and nothing else. Without it, a CSS fix could sit invisible behind
# a stale cache on the machines of the few people actually playing.
/src/*
  Cache-Control: public, no-cache

/*.html
  Cache-Control: public, no-cache

# Modest on purpose: no backend, no session, no cookie, so most of the usual list
# has nothing to protect here. These three do real work.
#   nosniff          the app serves JSON sets and ES modules; don't let a browser
#                    guess a content type for either
#   frame-options    nothing here should be framed by another site, least of all
#                    a page carrying creators' names and faces
#   referrer-policy  matches the referrerPolicy="no-referrer" already set on every
#                    card avatar — requests to Google's image CDN should not carry
#                    this site's URL, and this extends that to everything else
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
